        <body>
            <div xml:id="d24">

                <pb facs="0160" n="42" sameAs="frus:frus1889/pg42"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Count de Crenneville
                        to Mr. Wharton.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Austro-Hungarian Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1889-08-10"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 10, 1889</date>.
                        (Received August 12.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit to you a
                    letter addressed to the Austro-Hungarian consul in Milwaukee, by Mr. Bradley G.
                    Schley, relative to the Emerich Hasziel, who, on arriving with his family in New
                    York, was re-embarked on the steam-ship <hi rendition="#italic">Trave</hi> for
                    Bremen by the collector of the port.</p>
                <p>The two affidavits here inclosed show that Emerich Hasziel is not at all a
                    contract laborer, and that George Hasziel, his brother, who resides in Milwaukee
                    since a year and who is still an Austrian subject and a poor man, has paid the
                    amount of $124.50 for the tickets. This sum should be, under the circumstances,
                    repaid to him. I have therefore the honor to call your attention, sir, to this
                    matter, and begging you to inform me of the decision which your Government will
                    take, I renew, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">C. Crenneville.</hi></signed></closer>








